WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this morning we take you to one of the  last remnants of what new york was like  before new york city ever existed a  small old-growth forest found inside the  new york botanical garden it survived  everything from war to farming to real  estate development but now it faces a  new threat michael george takes us  inside  long before the crowds of times square  and the lights of broadway  this is what new york city looked like  and this is what it sounded like  a forest that has not been cut down  has been untouched by humankind  the thane family forest 50 acres of old  growth natural woods within the new york  botanical garden in the bronx  when the first leaves come out when do  the buds come out few people know the  magic of this place better than joanne  strauss a bronx native who's been coming  here since she was a young girl what  makes this place special  well just look around  you can't believe you're in the middle  of new york city you can't believe  you're in the bronx it's so peaceful  it's quiet  joanne still walks these same trails  today as a tour guide and a volunteer  studying the forest's ecosystem  i started volunteering here 20 years ago  i can't believe it it seems like  yesterday and i started in the master  composter program and so that's how i  got involved i  said i have to be here more often do you  feel that this kind of volunteer work is  more important now than ever yes  absolutely we want to educate people we  want to introduce people to the the  wonders of nature and how important it  is for us to preserve this natural place  and other natural places that we have  the forest is nestled between the bronx  river parkway and a metro north station  just over three miles from yankee  stadium but it's easy to miss even for  locals  do most new yorkers even know this  exists i don't think so because many  times people come here and they say i  grew up in new york all my life i had no  clue  the bronx river makes this place a  perfect refuge for native species like  owls foxes and migratory birds they're  trees that have been standing here since  the american revolution  but the history of this forest actually  predates america itself we should  acknowledge that we are on ancestral  land of the lenape people and we're  going up this ridge here elliott nagle  of the new york botanical garden is the  forest's director  so there is evidence to show that the  lenape used this land for hunting but we  don't besides that we don't know exactly  how they would have used this forest  specifically  when colonists arrived they quickly  found they couldn't use this land for  farming when you look through the site  it's very hilly and rocky that's one of  the reasons why up in through to the  development of the new york botanical  garden that the forest was preserved so  the forest remained undeveloped  natural  in 1895 the city formally set aside this  space entrusting its protection to the  botanical garden  is this forest the way it was hundreds  and hundreds of years ago absolutely not  and so that's one of the amazing things  to see is that our natural ecosystems  are constantly changing that interaction  with the city is causing all different  types of changes  time and time again invasive species  have threatened the ecosystem here it's  taken generations of work to protect the  native plants and animals and now  there's a new threat to the survival of  this place  severe weather events are already having  an impact  all these downed trees are from ida  absolutely yeah that's just some of the  impacts of our severe storms  when the remnants of hurricane ida  battered new york last september the  forest wasn't spared so when you talk  about threats to the forest like  invasive species like weather events  does climate change make those things  worse as you increase those weather  events and the severity of them then we  do increase the amount of disturbance in  the forest increasing canopy gaps but  also invasive species coming into those  areas and thriving and by monitoring a  forest like this we can monitor those  impacts we can identify management  practices to effectively treat them  before they even have an opportunity to  impact more of our larger rural areas  upstate  the hope is to ensure this place  continues to give us a living breathing  window into new york's past for  centuries to come  what's at stake if we lose this forest i  think it would be devastating look  around  inhale deeply you're just transported to  a different world here there's nothing  like being close to nature there's kind  of a quiet beauty to it that i think  everyone has the opportunity to come  here and explore and discover the site  for themselves and i hope they do  for cbs saturday morning michael george  the bronx

